Secured and monitored web infrastructure for www.foobar.com

Flow:
The user resolves www.foobar.com through the DNS (A record -> load
balancer IP) and connects over HTTPS on port 443. Firewall 1 lets the
traffic reach HAProxy, which holds the SSL certificate, decrypts the
request and forwards it to server 1 or server 2. Each server is protected
by its own firewall. A monitoring client on every server sends logs and
metrics to a monitoring service.

Why each new element:
- 3 firewalls (one per server): only allow the needed traffic (443 on the
  load balancer, load balancer traffic only on the web servers) and block
  everything else.
- 1 SSL certificate: serves www.foobar.com over HTTPS so traffic between
  the user and the load balancer is encrypted.
- 3 monitoring clients: collect logs and metrics on each server and send
  them to the monitoring service (e.g. Sumo Logic).

What firewalls are for:
A firewall filters incoming and outgoing traffic based on rules (IP,
port, protocol), reducing the attack surface.

Why HTTPS:
HTTPS encrypts data (passwords, cookies) so it cannot be read or modified
in transit, and the certificate proves to the user that the site is the
real www.foobar.com.

What monitoring is used for:
To know the health and performance of the system (uptime, load, errors,
resources) and to get alerts before users notice a problem.

How the monitoring tool collects data:
An agent installed on each server reads the log files and measures system
metrics (CPU, memory, disk, network), then sends them to the monitoring
service where they are stored, graphed and used for alerts.

Monitoring the web server QPS:
Enable Nginx's stub_status page (or parse the access log). The monitoring
client reads the request counter at regular intervals and computes the
queries per second, which is shown on a dashboard with an alert threshold.

Issues:
- SSL termination at the load balancer: traffic between the load balancer
  and the servers travels unencrypted (HTTP) inside the network, so
  anyone who gets into the internal network can read it.
- Only one MySQL server accepting writes: if the Primary fails no writes
  are possible, and all writes are limited to one machine.
- Servers with all the same components: database, web and application
  servers compete for the same CPU and memory, cannot be scaled
  separately, and one failing server takes several layers down at once.